NOTE: 

The examples in this directory are described in the userguide.

Short description
-----------------

foam1

 Stokes problem on a square domain with a circular bubble (void) at the center.
 There is surface tension on the bubble boundary.
 Inside the bubble a constant pressure is imposed and the domain boundaries
 remain straight, but can move according to a zero imposed force.
 A time stepping is performed where the nodes of the mesh are updated
 according to a Lagrangian scheme (forward Euler).
 Gmsh mesh.

foam2

 Stokes problem on a cubic domain with a spherical bubble (void) at the center.
 There is surface tension on the bubble boundary.
 Inside the bubble a constant pressure is imposed and the domain boundaries
 remain straight, but can move according to a zero imposed force.
 A time stepping is performed where the nodes of the mesh are updated
 according to a Lagrangian scheme (forward Euler).
 Gmsh mesh.

foam3

 Stokes problem on a square domain with a circular bubble (void).
 There is surface tension on the bubble boundary.
 Inside the bubble a constant pressure is imposed.
 Biperiodicity is imposed and the domain can freely stretch (without force)
 in x- and y-direction.
 A time stepping is performed where the nodes of the mesh are updated
 according to a Lagrangian scheme (forward Euler).
 Gmsh mesh.
 NOTE: this problem is similar to foam1, but now periodic conditions instead of
 symmetry conditions on the outer boundaries. The bubble can be placed
 off-center, but basically leads to the same problem.

foam4

 Stokes problem on a cubic domain with a spherical bubble (void).
 There is surface tension on the bubble boundary.
 Inside the bubble a constant pressure is imposed.
 Triperiodicity is imposed and the domain can freely stretch (without force)
 in x-, y- and z-direction.
 A time stepping is performed where the nodes of the mesh are updated
 according to a Lagrangian scheme (forward Euler).
 Gmsh mesh.
 NOTE: this problem is similar to foam2, but now periodic conditions instead of
 symmetry conditions on the outer boundaries. The bubble can be placed
 off-center, but basically leads to the same problem.
